{
  "rule": "What each CPython `ast` node kind is expected to be in our tree. A boundary comparison asks whether we have a node at these bytes; this asks whether it is the RIGHT node, which is the half a boundary check cannot see -- `foo();` parsed as a bodyless function declaration occupies exactly the bytes of the call it should be.",
  "semantics": "Several of our nodes can cover one span (a chain like `expression_statement > call_expression > identifier`), so an oracle node passes when ANY of our kinds at its span is listed. That is why only the CORE kind needs an entry and wrappers do not: `Call` lists `call_expression`, and the `expression_statement` around it comes along free. It also means the mapping is not required to be one-to-one -- what it is required to be is TOTAL and DECLARED. Every oracle kind the corpus produces has an entry, and anything not listed is reported rather than assumed.",
